## 1. Introduction
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are assembled from metal nodes (clusters), multi-functional organic linkers and they are extremely porous entities with huge surface area, tunable properties, controllable surface functionalities. They have been widely used in catalysis, gas storage, separation, biomedicine, drug delivery, sensing and biotech­ nology (Rasheed et al. 2020a, 2020b; Zhou et al., 2021). Applications of MOFs have been recently extended in sensing of different analytes in different fields like industrial, food, environmental, biomedical and various others. MOFs have insulating feature, due to this they have been explored in electrochemical sensing applications (Li et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2020). Promising applications of MOFs in sensing field is due to following features (a) presence of metal coordination sites, networking, large surface area, tunable porosity of MOFs made them superior in catalysis and as coating materials of electrodes employed in sensing applications (b) excellent porosity and surface area of MOFs are ad­ vantageous for transfer of analytes which effectively enhance the signal and sensing capability. Keeping in view these features of MOFs, Different molecules as glucose, harmful heavy metals, hydrogen peroxide, dyes, pharmaceuticals have been sensed by MOF based ma­ terials previously (Wang et al., 2018c). Introduction of MOFs with different functional entities bless the MOF materials with unique features to design different sensors with enhanced applications. Incor­ poration of various biomolecules (aptamers, enzymes and antibodies etc.) into MOFs expands scope of MOF based materials in biosensing field for detection of DNA, proteins etc. (An et al., 2019). Similarly nanomaterials based on carbons can be incorporated to MOFs which enhance the conductivity of MOFs leading to improvement in electro-activity of sensors (Bhardwaj et al., 2018). MOF based materials have been reported for sensing applications previously but yet there is need to provide insight in performance of different smart nanohybrid MOFs for extensive sensing of environmental pollutants and also other analytes. Here we have described in details the latest trends, advance­ ments and applications of MOFs incorporating biomolecules (enzymes, antibodies, aptamers and various carbon based materials (graphenes, fibers, carbon nanotubes etc.) in field of sensing.
## 2. Classification of MOFs
MOFs can be classified on the basis of synthetic methodology, framework and crystal structures. Among these parameters synthetic methodology is the promising tool to classify the MOFs. The various classes of MOFs include first, second and third generation MOFs. The first generation MOFs (also known as normal MOFs) involve organic and inorganic moieties in their basic framework, while second generation
MOFs are also known as functionalized MOFs and their surface is modified through different chemical modifications. Third generation MOFs are called as smart MOFs and they possess different biomolecules and gases in their architecture (Perry Iv et al., 2009). MOFs are also classified on basis of robustness of structural framework. The structure may be flexible or rigid. Flexible MOFs can change their orientation in under different conditions of temperature, pressure and pH. On the other hands, these factors did not affect the conformation of rigid MOFs (Liu et al., 2014). On basis of crystal structure arrangement, MOFs can be classified as crystalline and amorphous. Crystalline MOFs possess highly arranged solid structure porous architecture while amorphous MOFs have disordered structures and possess basic ligand and metal connec­ tivity (Bennett and Cheetham, 2014; Rasheed et al., 2020c).
## 3. Selection of MOFs for the sensing application
The sensitivity of the MOF based electrochemical sensor depends on active area of electrodes and mass transfer efficacy of analytes (Kem­ pahanumakkagari et al., 2018). Decrease in particle size gives nano­ materials which possess large electroactive surface area. MOFs with porous characteristics and novel structures (2D MOF nanosheets and hybrid structures) would enhance the accessibility of area of electrode and increase analyte diffusion. Thus nanoscale MOFs with novel struc­ tures and porous characteristics are effective in improving the sensor sensitivity. Nano MOFs are small in size and they possess large number of active sites. Wang and his colleagues reported the decrease in size of MOF from $1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ to $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ enhanced the catalytic capability of $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ –Ni-MOF by 2.7 fold (Wang et al., 2018b). Nano MOFs based sensors show increased sensitivity and low limit of detection. The porous MOFs combine the merits of macroporous and microporous MOFs and enhance the transference of electrons and mass diffusion thus leading to improvement of sensor sensitivity (Chen and Zhang, 2019). Synthesis of stable porous MOFs is always challenging. 2D MOFs possess large sur­ face area, easy diffusion, more active sites in comparison of 3D MOFs which leads to enhancement of sensor sensitivity (Wang et al., 2018b). Different methodologies have been reported for synthesis of 2D-nano­ sheets (Zhao et al., 2016). For ascorbic acid detection, ultrathin NI-MOF nanosheets have been synthesized through solvothermal method (Li et al., 2018c). Tuning of MOFs structure can enhance the sensitivity of sensors. Sensors based on pristine MOFs have many limi­ tations as low conductivity, aggregation and poor catalytic potential. Synthesis of MOF based hybrids by incorporating carbon materials, metal nanoparticles is an effective way to overcome these limitations (Muschi and Serre, 2019). Nano-hybrid architectures offer route be­ tween components to promote transference of electrons and inhibit aggregation of individual components and also provide more active sites. Zheng and his colleagues synthesized 2D MOF nano-films (thick­ ness $\mathsf{1}\mathrm{-}3\mathsf{m}\bar{}$ ) and these were further incorporated with carbon nanotubes, graphene oxides to develop hybrids (Bai et al., 2019). Hybrid MOF nano-film with CNT showed great response for detection of hydrogen peroxide.
## 4. MOF composites
Carbon based materials have excellent stability, good conductivity and their cost effectiveness which made them important to compositing with MOFs (Tong et al., 2020). Various 1D, 2D, 3D Carbon materials like carbon nanotubes (CNTs), carbon nanofibers, graphene nanosheets, graphene aerogels (Fu et al., 2018; Wu et al., 2019b; Xie et al., 2019)etc. have been used to fabricate MOF composites for their effective sensing applications (Fig. 1).
## 4.1. MOFs/Carbon based Composites
## 4.1.1. CNTs/MOFs
Hetero-structured Ni-MOF@CNTs hybrids was synthesized at room temperature. Ni-MOF contains high surface area, and good conductance of CNTs and the synergistic effect of Ni-MOF@CNTs hybrid exhibited excellent potential towards oxidation and electrochemical sensing of bisphenol A (BPA). Low limit of detection $(0.35\mathrm{nM})$ and high sensitivity $(284.64\upmu\mathrm{A}\upmu\mathrm{M}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ for BPA was obtained at Ni-MOF $@$ CNTs sensing interface $\mathrm{\DeltaTM}$ et al., 2020). Recently, Sn-based MOF was synthesized through solvothermal approach then further its composite with CNTs was prepared via sonochemical methodology. Sn-MOF crystallinity was retained on CNTs inclusion over the surface while great surface area and pore volume of Sn-MOF was exploited. The synthesized hybrid showed great potential for sensing of hydrogen peroxide with Low LOD ${\sim}4.7\times$ $10^{-3}\upmu\mathrm{M}$ along with linear range in between $0.2~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and $2.5\mathrm{mM}$ (Rani et al., 2020). So Sn-MOF/CNTs hybrid proved efficient for sensitivity. Composite of Ni-MOF with CNTs was fabricated through in-situ assem­ bly approach. The hybrid Ni-MOF/CNTs performed efficiently for glucose sensing with low LOD of $0.82\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and good sensitivity of 13.85 $\mathbf{\bar{m}A}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{M}^{-1}\mathbf{cm}^{-2}$ . Linear range was found $1\upmu\mathrm{M}{-}1.6\up m\mathrm{M}$ . This hybrid MOF showed great reproducibility, stability, selectivity repeatability and sensitivity as glucose sensor (Zhang et al., 2018c). Lu and his colleagues synthesized the Ni-MOF composite/Au NPs/CNTs/PVA composite to detect HIV DNA through DNA hybridization by employing methylene blue as redox indicator. Ni-MOF composite/Au NPs/CNTs/PVA com­ posite was deposited on cellulose membrane through vacuum filtration and drop coating approach to achieve film electrode. The obtained electrode made high loadings of probe DNA for its large area and con­ jugated $\pi$ -electron system that can give hydrogen bond to obtain inter­ action in between MOF and DNA which enhances the sensitivity for detection of target DNA. The variation of peak current for methylene blue adsorbed on DNA was observed (Fig. 2). Film maintained the electrochemical features after bending and stretching hundreds of times. The electrode showed great sensing potential with low LOD of $0.13\mathrm{nM}$ and linear range of $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ The HIV DNA was detected even in complex serum samples by using this film electrode. Thus facile, cost effective flexible MOF based electrodes can be used for determination of different pathogens (Lu et al., 2021).
In a research work, an electrochemical sensor was synthesized by combining of the MIL-53 which contains large surface area and good catalytic potential with CNTs to increase the conductivity and employing Prussian blue nanoparticles (electronic mediator) to enhance sensitivity (Duan et al., 2019). Pyrrole was used as conductive monomer and E2 was used as template for electro-polymerization to form conductive film to detect the $17\upbeta$ -estradiol with high selectivity and sensitivity with linear range of $10^{-14}$ to $10^{-9}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and detection limit of $6.19\times\mathrm{\dot{1}0^{-15}m o l\ L^{-1}}$ . Luteolin is biologically active component and is used extensively in medicinal applications. Recently MXene/ZIF-67/CNTs composite was fabricated for electrochemical sensing of Luteolin. Composite was designed by in-situ growth of MOF (ZIF-67) onto the surface of CNTs which confirms the stability and im­ proves the catalytic effect. MXene was employed as matrix for loading of ZIF-67/CNTs may enhance catalytic potential of the composite. MXene/ZIF-67/CNTs based modified electrode showed enhanced cata­ lytic potential and improved response to luteolin redox reaction. The fabricated sensor showed great performance with low LOD of $0.03\mathrm{nM}$ in linear range $(0.1\mathrm{-}1000\mathrm{nM})$ . Composite electrode showed high stability, selectivity and reproducibility (Xu et al., 2021) (Fig. 3). Cu-MOF/SWCNTs composite was fabricated and desposited on glass carbon electrode (GCE) and used as sensor for non-enzymatic detection of glucose in human saliva. The composite electrode exhibited sharp oxidation peak with improved current. The electrode determined glucose in linear range of $20\times10^{-9}\mathrm{M}$ to $80\times10^{-6}\mathrm{{M}}$ with LOD of $1.72\times$ $10^{-9}\mathbf{M}$ and sensitivity of $573\upmu\mathrm{A}\mathrm{mM}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Arul et al., 2020) (Fig. 4) (Table 1).
## 4.1.2. GO/MOFs
Keeping in view the 2-D graphene nanosheets/MOF composites different altered versions of graphene as GO (graphene oxide) (Wang et al., 2014), ERGO (electrochemically reduced Graphene oxide) (Yang et al., 2016), rGO (reduced Graphene oxide) (Ma et al., 2020; Zhang et al., 2018d), and graphene nanoribbons (GNRs) (Kung et al., 2016), have been employed in electrochemical sensing. Different techniques including in situ growth (Cao et al., 2019), solution diffusion (Zheng et al., 2017), ultrasound dispersion (Zhang et al., 2018d) and electro­ deposition (Wang et al., 2018a) to prepare MOFs/GN composites for the development of sensor. By utilizing the ultra-sonication strategy the Cu-TPA MOF/GO composites were synthesized and employed for the detection of dopamine and the acet-aminophen (Wang et al., 2014). HKUST-1/EGRO electrode was synthesized for detection of the di-hydroxybenzene isomers with great selectivity (Li et al., 2018a). MIL-101(Cr)/rGO composites were synthesized to detect 4-nonylphenol (Zhang et al., 2018d). While MOF/GN composites prepared through this easy ultra-sonication strategy were found unstable and not efficient for transfer of electrons. To solve this issue scientists (Cao et al., 2019) synthesized the HKUST-1/GN composites through in situ HKUST-1 growing onto liquid phase at room temperature exfoliated graphene nanosheets. With this approach the ultra-small HKUST-1 nanoparticles immobilized on GN nnao-sheets surfaces and dispersed homogeneously also. These synthesized composites showed great transfer electron capability and enhanced adsorption potential to 8-hydroxy-20-deoxy­ gua nosine therefore obtained great signals and detection capability. Sandwich kind ZIF-67/GN hybrids were synthesized through in situ liquid phase growth of ZIF-67 on GN nanosheets (acidified) (Chen et al., 2019b). To synthesize a sandwich kind ZIF-67/GN hetero framework, the ZIF-67 nanoparticles were homogeneously loaded on sides of GN nanosheets. In comparison of the ZIF-67 and $\mathrm{GN}+\mathrm{ZIF}–67$ mixture, the as-prepared ZIF-67/GN composite showed huge electrode active site and great capability to oxidation of glucose. Results showed that in situ synthetic strategy is advantageous in comparison of the physical strat­ egy this provides enhanced electrode conduction due to close interaction of the ZIF-67 and graphene. Consequently sensitive sensor for oxidation of glucose was prepared by employing as-prepared sandwich kind ZIF-67/GN hetero-framework with detection limit0.36 l $\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{S}/\mathbf{N}=3\$ ). To observe the various metal ions, the 3D graphene aerogel (GA)/MOF composites (UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{GA}\dot{}$ were explored (Lu et al., 2018) (Fig. 5). The UiO-6 ${6{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}}/{\mathrm{GA}}$ composites synthesized through in-situ growth of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystals onto GA framework in solvo-thermal conditions. The porous framework of UiO ${\cdot66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}/{\mathrm{GA}}$ composites having great surface area which provides high number of active sites. This composite showed great selectivity and sensitivity for detection of various metal ions $\mathrm{(Hg^{\overline{{2}}+}}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ ) individually or together. ZIF-8/3D graphene composites were fabricated through step by step process by the constant submerging graphene in zinc chloride media and ligand solutions at rt, and this approach synthesized sensors with enhanced efficacy for detection of DA (Zheng et al., 2017). Wang and co-workers (Xie et al., 2019) incorporated poly-pyrrole (as-linker) to increase binding between GAs and ZIF-8 and hetero-framework PPy@ZIF-8/GAs composites for detection of dichlorophenol ultra sensitively. Different carbon matrices as acetylene black (Wen et al., 2018), mesoporous carbon (Zhao et al., 2019b) and macroporous carbon (Cao et al., 2018) have been employed for usage as support platforms for MOFs (Table 1). MOF/carbon material composites showed that synchronization between framework and conductivities enhance the catalytic potential of com­ posite to various analytes. Recently through solvothermal approach, a composite MOF-818 $@$ RGO/MWCNTs was fabricated. This composite contained many active sites, porosity and good electrical conductivity and showed great electrocatalytic potential to various phenolic like caffeic acid, chlorogenic acid and Gallic acid. The sensor showed great sensitivity towards sensing of phenolic acids. This sensor may be used for determination of caffeic acid, chlorogenic acid and Gallic acid in real samples (Yan et al., 2020) (Fig. 6). Recently, graphitic carbon nitride functionalized with copper coordinated dithiooxamide metal organic framework $\mathrm{({g-C_{3}N_{4}/C u}}$ -DTO MOF) nanocomposite was fabricated. The synthesized nanocomposite electrode was used to determine an endo­ crine disruptor i.e., 4-(4-isopropoxy-benzenesulfonyl)-phenol (BPSIP). The LOD for BPSIP was found $0.02~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and sensitivity was 0.5675 $\upmu\mathrm{A}\upmu\mathrm{M}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . So the synthesized sensor could be used to determine BPSIP in real water samples (Singh et al., 2021). Habibi and his col­ leagues synthesized Cu-MOF/sulfur and nitrogen co-doped graphene (SNDGr) nanocompoiste on pencil graphite electrode (PGE) based sensor which was used to detect of sertraline hydrochloride (antide­ pressant drug). The as-synthesized sensor showed promising potential to detect sertraline hydrochloride with LOD of $0.038~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and in range of $0.05{-2.67~\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ with great sensitivity $(0.4557\upmu\mathrm{A}/\upmu\mathrm{M}\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ (Habibi et al., 2021).
## 4.1.3. MOF/miscellaneous carbon material composites
Carbon fiber/ZIF-67 composite was synthesized and effect of struc­ ture of carbon fiber like solid carbon fibers (SCF) and porous carbon fibers (PCF) on catalytic potential of was demonstrated ZIF-67 (Feng et al., 2017). Porous carbon fiber restricts aggregation and growth of ZIF-67 nano-crystals making ZIF-67/PCF composite contain more active sites, good conductance and more number of channels for transport. So resultantly ZIF-67/PCF composites showed great electro-catalytic sensing potential towards L-cysteine and nitro-benzene including less over-potential, broad detection limit and great stability. Hybrid of MOF-867 nanocrystals with pyrrole monomers was fabricated for sensing $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ metal. This as synthesized sensor showed good conduc­ tivity and showed promising detection of cadmium with LOD of $0.29\upmu\mathrm{g}$ ${\bf L}^{-1}$ (Li et al., 2021b). Different other carbon based materials like mac­ roporous, mesoporous carbon, acetylene black have been used as sup­ port for MOFs (Zhao et al., 2019b) (Table 1).
## 5. MOFs biocomposites
Selectivity, sensitivity and detection limit of electrochemical sensors which are based on MOF have been enhanced and improved by incor­ poration of antibodies, enzymes and nucleic acids into MOFs (Qiu et al., 2019). MOFs are porous materials with great surface area are considered as potential materials to immobilize the bio-molecules (An et al., 2019). Designing methods, cons and pros of various MOF based electrochemical enzyme, immuno and apta-sensors have been focused (Fig. 7).
## 5.1. MOF/enzyme biocomposites
Generally, on surface or inner pores of MOF materials the enzymes were immobilized (Drout et al., 2019). Through various methods including covalent-modification, physical-absorption and biological methods, the enzymes are stuffed on the MOF based surfaces. By using these approaches, enzymes can be directly available to bulk solution which allows unconstrained transferrnce of mass and that lead to greatly sensitive biosensors (Drout et al., 2019). For the purpose of direct and selective electrochemical sensing of pesticides and few other analytes, different MOF/enzyme biocomposites have been synthesized through surface absorption phenomena (Wang et al., 2019c). The boronic acid functionalized MIL-100(Cr)–B MOFs have been prepared through the metal-ligand fragment co-assembly approach (Dai et al., 2017). Robust binding of boric acid moieties onto MOFs and cis diol moieties on en­ zymes aided to immobilize various horse-radish peroxidase enzymes on the surfaces of MOF (Fig. 8).
This enhanced the sensitivity of projected sensor to trace hydrogen peroxides liberated from the living cells. This approach suffers from low strength, leakage and restricted loading due to great vulnerability of enzymes to surrounding environment. For practical application, the stability, structure and operation of biosensor may be enhanced through the enzymes en-capsulation in Cages of MOF. Chen and co-workers (Chen et al., 2017) synthesized $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ stable PCN 333 (Fe) MOF with meso-porous cages and high porosity and established encapsulation approach to synthesize MOF/enzyme bio-composites. Because of size match of big cavities of PCN-333 (Fe) MOF and horseradish peroxidase, the enzyme incorporation into MOF material recognized with great loading of enzyme $(0.25\mathrm{~g~g~}^{-1})$ . Horseradish peroxidase was uniformly distributed all over PCN-333(Fe) MOF and this was revealed in micro­ scopic image of labeled (fluorescent) HRP@PCN-333(Fe). Moreover, Shell of MOF also prohibited the leakage, structural changes and accu­ mulation of horseradish peroxidase throughout test that performed under acidic media at high temperature. The HRP@PCN-333(Fe) based biosensor responded to hydrogen peroxide with great sensitivity, supe­ rior thermal and operational stability that was found excellent in com­ parison of HRP surface adsorbed PCN-333(Fe) composites. These outcomes validated the benefits of enzyme@meso-MOF composites for formation of efficient and robust biosensors. Various MOFs are micro­ porous frameworks and small pores limit the selectivity of encapsulated enzymes and this give rise to reduced rate of diffusion (Doan et al., 2019). By keeping in view the concept of biomineralization, Li and his coworkers (Zhang et al., 2018a) synthesized glucose oxidase (GOx) $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{ZIF}{-8}$ biocomposites through in-situ growth of MOFs in existence of the GOx. This approach of in situ encapsulation bestowed the composite with great loading efficacy of $89\%$ GOx and fast capability of mass transference because of high porosity. After repeated testing in various conditions as heat, organic solvents and long storage the proposed sensor showed promising sensitivity and auspicious stability. Different enzyme ${\ @\mathrm{MOF}}$ based electrochemical biosensors have been reported in Table 2. In this methodology the small size of Pores of MOFs in com­ parison of enzymes to be encapsulated helps the enzymes to keep it away from leaching out of MOFs and also gives varied MOFs and enzymes biocomposites which may be employed for different applications. Apart from enzymes, few redox active species like hemin possessing excellent peroxidase potential also incorporated into the MOF material for determination of hydrogen peroxide and glucose etc (Cheng et al., 2018). Moreover, for enhancing the electron transfer efficacy between electrode and enzyme, the MOF/metal NP and MOF/Carbon composites have been employed to immobilize different enzymes for biosensors. Song and co-workers (Song et al., 2017) used ZIF-67/MWCNT as sup­ porting material to immobilize HRP for hydrogen peroxide determina­ tion. MWCNTs which are hydrophilic in nature possess excellent conductivities and responsible to augment the transport of electrons from enzymes to surface of electrodes and also provide promising at­ mosphere for enzymes, therefore enhancing the affinity between sub­ strate material and immobilized enzymes onto MOF. Similarly for sensing of glucose, the Tb-MOF/CNT composites were used to load glucose dehydrogenase (Song et al., 2017). For detection of hydrogen peroxide, the composite ZIF-8/GO/HRP has been synthesized (Fan et al., 2015). While for nitrile and hydrogen peroxide detection the Ag@Zn-MOF/GOx biocomposites have been prepared (Dong et al., 2016). Gao and co-workers synthesized biocomposite
Pt@UiO66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ /AChE (Fig. 9) for detection of pesticide (organo-phos phorus) (Ma et al., 2019b).
## 5.2. MOF/antibody biocomposites
Antibodies (Abs) have also been incorporated into MOFs to synthe­ size different immune-sensors with excellent selectivity and specificity. Antibody-conjugated MOFs were synthesized with post modification approach (Qiu et al., 2019). MOFs employment in immuo-sensors may be divided in two different parts comprising the MOFs application as outer-signal-probe for sandwich immune-sensors and MOFs applications as electrode-modifying materials for non-sandwich immune-sensors (Filik and Avan, 2019). For fabrication of sandwich kind immuno-sensors (electrochemical), employment of MOFs as outer signal probes has been reported during recent years (Table 2). Firstly, MOFs were used just as carrier to integrate redox species/bio-molecules for the generation and amplification of signals. Various noble metals based nanoparticles/quatum dots including ZIF-8 and MIL-101 employed to adorn the MOFs and used to immobilize the antibodies for particular identification of targets for purpose of amplification and carrying of signals (Zhong et al., 2019). Adorned noble metal based nanoparticles or quantum dots can enhance the loading of antibodies with -amino ter­ minals by their strong affinity of amino-M. Later on the MOFs (elec­ tro-active) as Copper based MOFS and Iron porphyrin based MOFs having great surface area used as nano-carriers and redox-mediators were employed directly in electrochemical sensors eluding post modi­ fication and incorporation of redox indicators (exogenous) (Han et al., 2017; Liu et al., 2016). For example copper based MOFs have been employed as signal-tag for determination of C-reactive proteins (CRP) through electrochemical immune-assay (Liu et al., 2016). Composites based on Gold/copper MOF (Au/Cu-MOF) have been employed to label the detection antibodies (Ab2). After occurrence of immuno-reaction (sandwich kind) between CRP and Ab2, massive copper-MOFs added on surface of electrode, producing robust signal outputs. The copper ions $(\mathsf{C u}^{+2})$ signals produced through MOFs may be perceived directly without dissolution of acid and pre-concentration which potentially simplify the determination process and increase the detection efficacy. Further-more 3 Dimensional enhanced MOF possess vast quantity of copper ions producing efficient signals. Ions of metals play role as active (redox) centers, thereof any variation in valence and oxidation potential of metal ions may degrade the MOF. For solving this issue chen and co-workers (Han et al., 2017) prepared ferrocene (Fc) adorned $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF (Fc-Zn-MOF) for usage as signal (electro-chemical) probe for detection of the amyloid-b through immune-assay (electro-chemical). Great con­ tent Fc signal units covalently restrained in $\mathrm{Zn{\cdot}B D C{\cdot}N H_{2}}$ MOF through amide reaction which inhibit the leaching of signal molecules leading to sensor with improved electro-chemical efficiency and stability in strong harsh environment. As per above presented study the assembly of sandwich immune-sensors was found expensive and quite complex. So the fabrication of label free non-sandwich immune-sensor is required for potential applications because of their easy operation, economical and excellent efficacy (Liu et al., 2018). Easy and label free immune-sensor have been proposed (Li et al., 2019) by employing cobalt MOFs as car­ riers to anchor great catalytic capability CuAu NWs, and then antibodies immobilized onto MOFs for detection of nuclear matrix protein-22 (NMP-22). Cobalt-MOFs/Cu–Au NW nanocomposite quickly catalyzes the hydrogen peroxide oxidation in existence of hydrogen peroxide and produces an electrochemical signal output. With Enhancing concentra­ tion of NMP-22 antigen particularly affixed on immuno-sensor, the current affiliated with hydrogen peroxide response reduced through changing degrees because of obstacle of immobilized NMP-22 antigen for hydrogen peroxide reduction electrocatalytically. In optimal condi­ tions, a wide detection range and less detection limit down to $33\mathrm{fg}\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ concentrations of NMP-22 was showed by the immune-sensor. AuNPs/PtNPs/Fe-MOFs nanomaterials have been prepared (Zhao et al., 2019a) for label free electrochemical immuo-sensor preparation.
Sensitive impedimetric electrochemical immunosensor have been fabricated (Liu et al., 2017a) for sensing vomitoxin (VOM) by employing a greatly stable Al-MOF. On the gold electrode, firstly Al-MOF was immobilized and then MOF adsorbed the antibodies. Because of pres­ ence of vomitoxin the great binding affinity between VOM and anti­ bodies enhanced the electro-chemical impedance due to insulating characteristics of organic compounds which restricted the electron transfer of $[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]^{3-/4-}$ in electrolyte towards surface of electrode. EIS signal enhanced with increased concentration of vomitoxin. The varia­ tion in electron transfer resistance of immuno-sensor was employed as out-put signal to determine the sensor response towards various vomi­ toxin concentrations. At $\mathrm{pg~mL^{-1}}$ levels the vomitoxin can be detected with this methodology. The Cu-MOF/PANI composites integrated with anti-E. coli antibodies have fabricated for sensing the $E$ . coli (Gupta et al., 2019).
## 5.3. MOFs/aptamer biocomposites
Aptamers are artificially developed nucleic acids having great sta­ bility, specificity, alterations and they possess flexibility to detect various targets including drug molecules, metal ions, proteins etc (Cheng et al., 2009). In comparison of antibody immune-sensors the aptamer based sensors has various benefits including great sensitivity, cost effective, excellent selectivity and great chemical and thermal sta­ bility (Willner and Zayats, 2007). Aptasensors attracted great attention and various nanomaterials including polymers, MNPs, carbon matrices have been employed as immobilization platforms to increase the loading quantity and biological potential of aptamer strands (Cheng et al., 2009; Lv et al., 2020; Xu et al., 2009). Similarly to structure of MOF immune-sensors, MOFs particularly amino-MOFs $\mathrm{(NH}_{2}$ -MOFs) including PCN-224, UiO- ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu-BDC-NH_{2}}$ , may be employed to immo­ bilizing the signal molecule and aptamers performing as signal probes to form sandwich kind aptasensors (Sun et al., 2019; Wu et al., 2019a). MOF-based label free electrochemical aptasensors are cost effective and have been studied systematically (Liu et al., 2017b; Su et al., 2017). On basis of the EIS (electro-chemical impedance strategy), label-free elec­ trochemical aptasensor have been developed for detection of the lyso­ zyme by employing zirconium based MOF (Zr-MOF) (Liu et al., 2017b). Three various $z\mathrm{r}$ based MOFs with variable tunable pore characteristics through terminus ligands (HBA, HNA, and H3TATB) in channels have been developed and employed to composites with the aptamers and to prepare aptasensors for detection of proteins. From these Zirconium based MOFs composites the Zr- MOF-BA exhibited excellent detection efficacy and great electron trasferrance variation before and after determination and $z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF-TATB showed second good performed and Zr-MOF-NA was found poorest in performance. Mechanistic studies showed that various pore sizes and micro-environment of zirconium based MOFS provide varied binding performances of aptamers and targeted proteins that may be accountable for changed capability of sensors. In $z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF-BA, onto the surfaces of MOF the aptamers were immobilized with vertical alignments and $z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF-BA sustained the great biological activity in comparison of the various different com­ posites for binding with targets; though starnds of aptamers placed on surface of $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF-NA which led to poor binding with lysozymes, while for $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF-TATB very weak interaction noticed between framework and the sequences of aptamers which could be because of small size of pores of framework providing aptamers exfoliating in medium. Through In-situ incorporation strategy various $z\mathbf{r}$ based MOF/aptamer composites have been prepared that endorsed aptamers to embed in Zr based MOFs homogeneously and enhancing the loading efficacy of aptamers (Zhang et al., 2017c). The as-synthesized aptasensors proved greatly effective with ultra-sensitivity to detect carcino-embryonic, thrombin and kana­ mycin antigens. For detection of the MUC1, 2-D zirconium based MOF nanosheets were developed and employed as aptasensors (He et al., 2017). The novel thin framework increased the efficacy and surface concentration of aptamers. By combining surface resonance spectroscopy (SRP) and electrochemical techniques to review the ki­ netics of immobilization phenomena of aptamers and MUC1 detection. Moreover gold nanoparticles incorporated in $z\mathbf{r}$ based MOF nanosheets to sensitively determine cocaine even at trace levels (pM level) (Su et al., 2017). The Active sites which interacts with aptamers were enhanced by incorporation of gold nanoparticles. For detection of the heavy metal ions at trace levesl the nanostructural composites comprising Fe-MOFs and $\mathrm{mFe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{mC}$ $(\mathrm{Fe-MOF@mFe_{3}O_{4}@m C})$ were synthesized (Zhang et al., 2017d). Incorporation of $\mathrm{mFe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{mC}$ with pore containing framework enhances the adsorption potential and dispersion of Fe-MOF permitting aptamers immobilize and penetrate on surface of nano­ composites. Some of this kind of research is summarized in Table 2.
## 6. Conclusion and future perspectives
MOF and its composites have been reported as advanced electro­ chemical sensing platforms. In this review the advances in MOF based materials in sensing applications are of topic of interest and discussed in details. Also the structural features and approaches for increasing sta­ bility, sensitivity and selectivity of MOFs and their derivatives based sensors have been presented. Further, the combination of MOFs with carbon nanomaterials promote the active sites, enhance conductivity and increase sensitivity of sensors. While the introduction of bio­ molecules like enzymes, aptamers and antibodies in MOFs would be promising to enhance the sensor selectivity. Though field of MOFs based sensors is developed but still there are various challenges which are yet to be resolved for advancement of MOF based hybrids for sensing ap­ plications. Mechanism through which MOF hybrids sense analytes is not properly understood yet. So there is need to understand the mechanism properly to explore the promising applications of MOF hybrids in various fields for sensing of various analytes. For MOF/enzyme, MOF/ antibodies and MOF/aptamers, the enzymes, antibodies and aptamers serve as sensing sites while MOFs give framework with great surface area, diffusion and selectivity for immobilization of biomolecules. For MOF hybrids, control of MOFs assembly at molecular level is of great importance to understand the synergy which results from MOF com­ posites generation. Inspite of many challenges, MOF based composites are promising for sensing applications in various fields.
